https://www.khronos.org/opengl/wiki/Geometry_Shader

Definitely a good start.

Primarily good for 2 reasons:

[a] Layered Rendering -> output same primitive to multiple images using different layers.
Can be done in two ways:
 - loop inside the GS and do multiple emit_primitive
 - use GS instancing where separate GS instances pass you the gl_invocationid and you can use this to select the layerID
 Where supported in hardware, the latter is preferred since you get parallelism across separate instances

[b] Custom Culling
The first stage where you have complete primitive information. So you can really reject certain primitives using custom culling techniques

[c] Transform Feedback
http://rastergrid.com/blog/2010/10/gpu-based-dynamic-geometry-lod/

This guy uses GS to pick the correct primitive LOD based on distance and streams it to different Transform Feedback streams. These streams can later be played back.

Bottomline, if you ignore the Dynamic Geomoetry LOD part, this lets you do frustum culling, and pipe to TF streams. You can thus avoid doing complex asynchronous queries.



